WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.664
Welcome to Module 8 of the
Provider Notices learning path:

2
00:00:04.664 --> 00:00:06.875
digest, evidence and go/no-go.

3
00:00:06.875 --> 00:00:09.075
Most notices shouldn't be tasks.

4
00:00:09.075 --> 00:00:12.109
The weekly digest is how
the owners see the rest,

5
00:00:12.109 --> 00:00:14.987
and how they see what
needs action and by when.

6
00:00:14.987 --> 00:00:17.582
The evidence is what the ISSO keeps:

7
00:00:17.582 --> 00:00:23.129
every run recorded with the controls and
K S I's it supports, every file's hash,

8
00:00:23.129 --> 00:00:28.676
and the hash of the record before it, so
a changed file or a removed week shows.

9
00:00:28.676 --> 00:00:33.508
This module is Gate 7 of the provider
notices implementation guide, with Chapter

10
00:00:33.508 --> 00:00:38.125
7 of the developer workbook, and the
deployment runbook's promotion criteria.

11
00:00:38.125 --> 00:00:39.913
It takes about sixty minutes.

12
00:00:39.913 --> 00:00:44.070
You'll write a week's digest,
record three runs, check the chain,

13
00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:49.225
see a lab stage fail the go/no-go, and
catch a file edited after it was recorded.

14
00:00:49.225 --> 00:00:55.090
The code is illustrative: tested against
fixtures, not run against a live tenant.

15
00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:01.103
Why a digest, and why evidence?

16
00:01:01.103 --> 00:01:04.414
Not every notice is a task,
and most shouldn't be.

17
00:01:04.414 --> 00:01:07.065
The digest is how the owners see the rest.

18
00:01:07.065 --> 00:01:10.500
Once a week it lists, for each
provider, the open notices;

19
00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:14.164
every notice that needs action,
with its due date and owner;

20
00:01:14.164 --> 00:01:18.056
the maintenance and changes
starting in the next thirty days;

21
00:01:18.056 --> 00:01:22.178
what's new in the last week; and
what closed or stopped being listed.

22
00:01:22.178 --> 00:01:25.389
It goes to the owners,
not to a shared mailbox.

23
00:01:25.389 --> 00:01:30.183
The Compliance Automation and Day-Two
Operations book gives it a natural home in

24
00:01:30.183 --> 00:01:31.828
reviews that already exist:

25
00:01:31.828 --> 00:01:36.621
the service desk supervisor's weekly review
of the queue, and the change advisory

26
00:01:36.621 --> 00:01:41.128
board's agenda, where the next thirty
days of maintenance and changes belong.

27
00:01:41.128 --> 00:01:43.782
Two numbers from it are
worth watching over time:

28
00:01:43.782 --> 00:01:48.097
notices that needed action and reached
their due date with the task still open,

29
00:01:48.097 --> 00:01:51.615
and notices that were never routed
because no rule matched them.

30
00:01:51.615 --> 00:01:53.153
That's design guidance.

31
00:01:53.153 --> 00:01:57.081
The evidence is the other
half: what the ISSO keeps,

32
00:01:57.081 --> 00:02:00.120
recorded so a change or a gap shows.

33
00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:06.783
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module.

34
00:02:06.783 --> 00:02:11.232
You'll write the weekly digest, and read
its five sections as an owner would.

35
00:02:11.232 --> 00:02:15.983
You'll record a collection, a
routing run and a digest as evidence,

36
00:02:15.983 --> 00:02:20.107
and say which steps, controls
and K S I's each record names.

37
00:02:20.107 --> 00:02:23.074
You'll check the evidence
chain and the checksum file,

38
00:02:23.074 --> 00:02:25.770
and show that an edit
after recording fails both.

39
00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:27.525
You'll check a stage's coverage,

40
00:02:27.525 --> 00:02:30.895
and name the steps a test stage
and a production stage need.

41
00:02:30.895 --> 00:02:34.516
And you'll read the runbook's
promotion go/no-go criteria,

42
00:02:34.516 --> 00:02:37.507
and say which ones the
evidence check answers.

43
00:02:37.507 --> 00:02:43.158
You need Module 7 first, because this lab
repeats its two collections and routings.

44
00:02:43.158 --> 00:02:48.457
Lab 1 needs PowerShell 7.1 or
later with Pester 5, and no tenant.

45
00:02:48.457 --> 00:02:54.470
Lab 2 uses a run folder from an earlier
module's Lab 2, kept on agency storage.

46
00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:00.495
Unit 1 is the digest as code.

47
00:03:00.495 --> 00:03:04.075
The digest script reads a
collection and the routing state,

48
00:03:04.075 --> 00:03:06.120
and writes a page in five sections.

49
00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:11.458
One: open notices by provider, counted
by kind and by action required.

50
00:03:11.458 --> 00:03:15.555
Two: action required, soonest
due first, with the assignment

51
00:03:15.555 --> 00:03:19.820
group the routing named, or the
word digest when there's no task.

52
00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:23.833
Three: maintenance and changes
starting in the next thirty days.

53
00:03:23.833 --> 00:03:26.192
Four: what's new in the last seven days,

54
00:03:26.192 --> 00:03:28.945
by the first-seen time
in the routing state.

55
00:03:28.945 --> 00:03:32.868
And five: what closed or stopped
being listed in the last seven days,

56
00:03:32.868 --> 00:03:36.220
including notices the provider
simply stopped listing.

57
00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:39.060
It writes the page as
Markdown for the owners,

58
00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:43.358
a spreadsheet file with each notice's
route and owner, and a manifest.

59
00:03:43.358 --> 00:03:45.290
The window lengths are parameters,

60
00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:49.220
and in the scheduled run they come
from the settings file's digest block.

61
00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:52.201
The guide's validation
for the digest is simple:

62
00:03:52.201 --> 00:03:56.240
every action-required notice
shows its due date and owner.

63
00:03:59.990 --> 00:04:02.615
Unit 2 starts with what
the guide leaves to you.

64
00:04:02.615 --> 00:04:07.095
The guide says the digest goes to
the owners, but owners read a page,

65
00:04:07.095 --> 00:04:11.078
not a JSON file, so the kit
writes a page and a spreadsheet.

66
00:04:11.078 --> 00:04:13.627
It says every run is in the evidence,

67
00:04:13.627 --> 00:04:18.814
but a folder of files can be changed
after the fact, so the kit checks each

68
00:04:18.814 --> 00:04:24.177
run folder against its manifest, copies
it, and records every file's SHA-256.

69
00:04:24.177 --> 00:04:26.584
It lists controls and K S I's per gate,

70
00:04:26.584 --> 00:04:31.479
but someone has to say which run
supports which, so each record names its

71
00:04:31.479 --> 00:04:36.291
steps and their controls and K S I's,
from the kit's requirements catalog.

72
00:04:36.291 --> 00:04:41.142
Records are kept over time, but a record
removed from the middle goes unnoticed,

73
00:04:41.142 --> 00:04:44.352
so each record holds the hash
of the record before it.

74
00:04:44.352 --> 00:04:46.372
And the guide asks for a go/no-go,

75
00:04:46.372 --> 00:04:50.491
which raises the question of
which steps still lack evidence.

76
00:04:50.491 --> 00:04:53.215
The stage check answers it, step by step.

77
00:04:53.215 --> 00:04:58.540
The workbook's verdict: a list of files
isn't evidence until someone can check it.

78
00:05:02.290 --> 00:05:05.365
The evidence script records
one run folder at a time.

79
00:05:05.365 --> 00:05:10.603
It re-hashes every file the run's manifest
lists, copies the folder under runs,

80
00:05:10.603 --> 00:05:15.841
and writes a record with the time, the
environment, the mode, the change record,

81
00:05:15.841 --> 00:05:21.328
the command, and the result, which is pass
only if every file matches its manifest.

82
00:05:21.328 --> 00:05:27.424
The record names the steps the run
covers, with their controls and K S I's,

83
00:05:27.424 --> 00:05:33.627
the claim language, every file's SHA-256,
and the previous record's SHA-256.

84
00:05:33.627 --> 00:05:36.983
It appends an index file
and rewrites a checksum

85
00:05:36.983 --> 00:05:40.340
file that the standard
sha256sum tool can check.

86
00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:44.790
A collection covers one collection step
per provider and the normalize step.

87
00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:49.952
A routing run covers route, and contingency
if a payload asks for a plan review.

88
00:05:49.952 --> 00:05:51.914
A digest covers digest.

89
00:05:51.914 --> 00:05:54.376
The runbook's pre-flight, scheduled run,

90
00:05:54.376 --> 00:05:57.452
alert plan and rollback
each have their own step.

91
00:05:57.452 --> 00:06:00.664
The check verifies the
checksums and walks the chain.

92
00:06:00.664 --> 00:06:04.586
With a stage, it adds a row
for each step that stage needs:

93
00:06:04.586 --> 00:06:10.000
test needs pre-flight, run, route and
digest, and production adds alerts.

94
00:06:13.750 --> 00:06:18.998
The runbook promotes to production only
when seven criteria pass, and the ISSO and

95
00:06:18.998 --> 00:06:24.400
the change advisory board record the decision
with the test stage's evidence folder.

96
00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:28.838
One: the test stage's evidence
passes the stage check, every row.

97
00:06:28.838 --> 00:06:32.156
Two: every enabled provider's
collection matched the

98
00:06:32.156 --> 00:06:35.475
provider's own command,
recorded in the change record.

99
00:06:35.475 --> 00:06:37.712
Three: the smoke test passed.

100
00:06:37.712 --> 00:06:39.783
Four: the routing was reviewed,

101
00:06:39.783 --> 00:06:43.925
and every security advisory and
retirement routed to a task.

102
00:06:43.925 --> 00:06:48.513
Five: the alert policy applied,
and its rollback was rehearsed.

103
00:06:48.513 --> 00:06:51.887
Six: each provider's
notice contacts are named.

104
00:06:51.887 --> 00:06:56.296
And seven: the known gaps are recorded
with the ISSO, such as Google

105
00:06:56.296 --> 00:07:01.113
Cloud's planned maintenance and any
AWS account without a support plan.

106
00:07:01.113 --> 00:07:04.538
The evidence check answers
the first criterion directly.

107
00:07:04.538 --> 00:07:07.524
The chapter's dangers explain
the rest of the design.

108
00:07:07.524 --> 00:07:13.000
A weekly run that stops silently shows as
a gap in dates and a missing run record.

109
00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:15.362
A file edited later fails its hash.

110
00:07:15.362 --> 00:07:17.551
A removed record breaks the chain.

111
00:07:17.551 --> 00:07:22.041
And a pass read as a control decision
is guarded by each record's claim:

112
00:07:22.041 --> 00:07:25.710
it supports the controls,
and the assessor decides.

113
00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:30.185
Try it.

114
00:07:30.185 --> 00:07:34.229
Lab 1 runs in the provider
notices folder of the examples,

115
00:07:34.229 --> 00:07:37.585
in PowerShell 7, on the
kit's fictional fixtures.

116
00:07:37.585 --> 00:07:41.970
Step one repeats Module 7's two
collections and routings in one go:

117
00:07:41.970 --> 00:07:45.706
twenty-two notices and twelve
creates in the first week,

118
00:07:45.706 --> 00:07:51.147
then twenty-three notices and one create,
four updates and one close in the second.

119
00:07:51.147 --> 00:07:56.765
Step two writes the digest for the second
week, as of November sixteenth, 2026:

120
00:07:56.765 --> 00:08:02.203
sixteen open, eight that need action,
five starting in the next thirty days,

121
00:08:02.203 --> 00:08:05.323
two new, and four closed
or no longer listed.

122
00:08:05.323 --> 00:08:07.986
Step three reads it as the owners will.

123
00:08:07.986 --> 00:08:13.184
In action required, soonest first, the
EC2 instance retirement on December first,

124
00:08:13.184 --> 00:08:18.615
the Oracle Cloud Infrastructure certificate
bundle change on December fifteenth,

125
00:08:18.615 --> 00:08:22.262
and so on to the Azure retirement
on September thirtieth,

126
00:08:22.262 --> 00:08:24.822
2027, each with the
group it was routed to.

127
00:08:24.822 --> 00:08:28.073
The two with no date sit at
the bottom as not stated.

128
00:08:28.073 --> 00:08:31.623
And the Google Cloud row of
the first table is all zeros,

129
00:08:31.623 --> 00:08:34.270
because both its incidents closed.

130
00:08:38.020 --> 00:08:40.842
Step four records three runs as evidence:

131
00:08:40.842 --> 00:08:44.908
the second week's collection,
its routing run, and the digest.

132
00:08:44.908 --> 00:08:46.058
Each passes.

133
00:08:46.058 --> 00:08:50.170
The collection covers all five
providers and the normalize step.

134
00:08:50.170 --> 00:08:54.948
The routing run covers route and
contingency, and so it adds C P two and the

135
00:08:54.948 --> 00:09:00.045
recovery planning K S I, because a payload
asked for a contingency plan review.

136
00:09:00.045 --> 00:09:02.070
The digest covers digest.

137
00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:05.882
Each record also names
its controls and K S I's.

138
00:09:05.882 --> 00:09:10.577
Step five checks the chain: twenty-five
files match the checksum file,

139
00:09:10.577 --> 00:09:13.295
and the three records' chain is unbroken.

140
00:09:13.295 --> 00:09:16.107
Step six checks the test stage's coverage.

141
00:09:16.107 --> 00:09:19.776
The checksums and chain
pass, route and digest pass,

142
00:09:19.776 --> 00:09:23.008
but pre-flight and run
fail: no passing record.

143
00:09:23.008 --> 00:09:24.508
The command exits one.

144
00:09:24.508 --> 00:09:25.346
That's right.

145
00:09:25.346 --> 00:09:31.332
A lab has no pre-flight or scheduled run,
so the go/no-go's first criterion fails.

146
00:09:31.332 --> 00:09:33.169
Module 9 runs both.

147
00:09:33.169 --> 00:09:36.178
Step seven reads the records' index:

148
00:09:36.178 --> 00:09:40.480
three rows, collect, route
and digest, each pass.

149
00:09:44.230 --> 00:09:47.055
Step eight is the one an
assessor cares about.

150
00:09:47.055 --> 00:09:49.258
You add a line to the recorded digest,

151
00:09:49.258 --> 00:09:52.930
as if someone edited it after it
was recorded, and check again.

152
00:09:52.930 --> 00:09:55.542
Both checks fail, and both name the file.

153
00:09:55.542 --> 00:09:58.231
The checksum row says the digest changed.

154
00:09:58.231 --> 00:10:02.255
The chain row says the digest no
longer matches the third record.

155
00:10:02.255 --> 00:10:03.892
And the command exits one.

156
00:10:03.892 --> 00:10:08.532
That's the guard against the workbook's
danger of a recorded file edited later:

157
00:10:08.532 --> 00:10:12.592
the evidence would no longer show
what was read, and the check says so.

158
00:10:12.592 --> 00:10:15.965
The same design catches a
record removed from the middle,

159
00:10:15.965 --> 00:10:19.193
because each record holds the
hash of the one before it.

160
00:10:19.193 --> 00:10:24.743
Step nine runs the kit's whole test
suite: one hundred forty pass, none fail.

161
00:10:24.743 --> 00:10:28.101
The tests cover the digest's
sections and counts,

162
00:10:28.101 --> 00:10:32.467
the record's fields and claim
language, the chain, a changed file,

163
00:10:32.467 --> 00:10:36.330
a removed record, a failed run,
and a stage's missing steps.

164
00:10:36.330 --> 00:10:38.530
Then delete the output folder.

165
00:10:42.280 --> 00:10:45.930
Lab 2 does the same with a
real test environment's runs.

166
00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:50.682
You need the run folder from an earlier
module's Lab 2, in a test tenant,

167
00:10:50.682 --> 00:10:55.518
account, tenancy or project, never
production, kept on agency storage.

168
00:10:55.518 --> 00:10:57.696
You also need a new evidence folder,

169
00:10:57.696 --> 00:11:01.379
a new routing state file, and
the test change record's number.

170
00:11:01.379 --> 00:11:04.867
Route and digest the test
collection with the lab's routing.

171
00:11:04.867 --> 00:11:08.817
Record all three runs with the
change record, and check the chain.

172
00:11:08.817 --> 00:11:13.604
Then do the step no script can: give the
digest to the test environment's owners,

173
00:11:13.604 --> 00:11:17.717
and ask whether each action-required
row reached the right person.

174
00:11:17.717 --> 00:11:22.084
To back it out, keep the evidence of
the test stage with its change record,

175
00:11:22.084 --> 00:11:25.192
and delete only the copies
outside agency storage.

176
00:11:25.192 --> 00:11:27.342
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

177
00:11:27.342 --> 00:11:30.910
The workbook's Chapter 7
has the exact commands.

178
00:11:34.660 --> 00:11:36.047
The federal requirement.

179
00:11:36.047 --> 00:11:42.711
Gate 7 supports NIST special publication
eight hundred fifty-three S I five, part c;

180
00:11:42.711 --> 00:11:44.965
S A nine, part c; R A three, part f,

181
00:11:44.965 --> 00:11:50.550
updating the risk assessment when
the environment changes; C A seven,

182
00:11:50.550 --> 00:11:56.135
continuous monitoring; and A U nine,
protection of audit information.

183
00:11:56.135 --> 00:12:01.047
The pre-flight's named contacts support
S R eight, notification agreements.

184
00:12:01.047 --> 00:12:04.998
The digest, each week, is the
evidence for the first three.

185
00:12:04.998 --> 00:12:09.953
The records and their index, with every
file's hash and the previous record's hash,

186
00:12:09.953 --> 00:12:14.534
support continuous monitoring and
the protection of audit information.

187
00:12:14.534 --> 00:12:17.406
The checksum file and the
check show the folder is

188
00:12:17.406 --> 00:12:20.347
unchanged since it was
recorded, and catch a change.

189
00:12:20.347 --> 00:12:24.697
The stage check shows each step has
a passing record, or names the gap.

190
00:12:24.697 --> 00:12:29.122
Your organization still sets the
parameters, and the assessor decides.

191
00:12:29.122 --> 00:12:33.761
K S I's are FedRAMP twenty X indicators
for cloud service providers.

192
00:12:33.761 --> 00:12:37.677
The agency lines its evidence up
with them and doesn't report them,

193
00:12:37.677 --> 00:12:41.080
and each record carries
that sentence as its claim.

194
00:12:44.830 --> 00:12:46.805
Check yourself with two questions.

195
00:12:46.805 --> 00:12:51.980
First: a lab's evidence passes the check,
but fails the check for the test stage.

196
00:12:51.980 --> 00:12:52.668
Why?

197
00:12:52.668 --> 00:12:53.893
The answer is b.

198
00:12:53.893 --> 00:12:57.315
The test stage needs passing
pre-flight and run records,

199
00:12:57.315 --> 00:13:01.406
from the pre-flight and the scheduled
run, and a lab doesn't make them.

200
00:13:01.406 --> 00:13:02.755
Module 9 does.

201
00:13:02.755 --> 00:13:06.342
Second: every row of the
production stage check passes.

202
00:13:06.342 --> 00:13:07.917
What does that tell the agency?

203
00:13:07.917 --> 00:13:09.142
The answer is b.

204
00:13:09.142 --> 00:13:12.790
Every step the production stage
needs has a passing record,

205
00:13:12.790 --> 00:13:15.618
in an unbroken chain,
with matching checksums.

206
00:13:15.618 --> 00:13:19.305
The records support the controls,
and the assessor decides them.

207
00:13:19.305 --> 00:13:22.636
A pass isn't a control
decision, and it doesn't mean

208
00:13:22.636 --> 00:13:26.717
FedRAMP approved anything or
that the agency reports K S I's.

209
00:13:26.717 --> 00:13:29.145
The module page has three more questions,

210
00:13:29.145 --> 00:13:32.000
each linked to the
section that answers it.

211
00:13:35.750 --> 00:13:36.575
To sum up.

212
00:13:36.575 --> 00:13:40.763
You wrote a week's digest, recorded
three runs as chained evidence,

213
00:13:40.763 --> 00:13:45.728
checked the chain, saw a lab stage fail the
go/no-go for the two steps it never ran,

214
00:13:45.728 --> 00:13:48.675
and caught a file edited
after it was recorded.

215
00:13:48.675 --> 00:13:52.962
The evidence an ISSO keeps for
Gate 7 is each week's digest,

216
00:13:52.962 --> 00:13:58.709
as a page and a spreadsheet file; the
evidence folder's records, runs, index and

217
00:13:58.709 --> 00:14:04.638
checksum file; and, at promotion, the stage
check's output with the go/no-go record.

218
00:14:04.638 --> 00:14:07.713
Before shipping, work through
the chapter's checklist.

219
00:14:07.713 --> 00:14:12.851
Next, and optional, is Module 9:
deploying to test and production.

220
00:14:12.851 --> 00:14:14.800
It's for whoever deploys the kit.

221
00:14:14.800 --> 00:14:18.433
It records a pre-flight and a
weekly run, so the stage check that

222
00:14:18.433 --> 00:14:22.350
failed here passes, and then takes
the same commands to production.

223
00:14:22.350 --> 00:14:24.200
Or go back to the learning path.
